    {
      "title": "Redirected",
      "desc": "The wrong answer gives it away",
      "detail": "Instead of the test text, the \"server\" answers 302 Found, pointing to the router’s login page. Its TTL is 64, not lowered at all, because it came straight from the router. Windows sees the wrong answer, marks the network as needing action and opens the browser, whose first request the router redirects the same way, to its login page. This only works with plain HTTP: the router can’t fake an HTTPS page without a certificate error.",
      "facts": [
        [
          "Answer",
          "302 → 192.168.1.1/login"
        ],
        [
          "Give-away",
          "TTL 64, from one hop away"
        ],
        [
          "Why HTTP",
          "HTTPS can’t be faked"
        ]
      ],
      "events": [
        {
          "type": "packet",
          "from": "router",
          "to": "client",
          "color": "violet",
          "label": "302 FOUND",
